  <title>7.17.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02042</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.17.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>875b16ef-4bbf-4670-a865-311d44f0d97d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Here is the graph of quadratic function \(f\).</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f6d2f05756f240bf0fbb2729b1726aedba9ccbb7"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Andre uses the expression \((x−5)^2+7\) to define \(f\).</para>
<para>Noah uses the expression \((x+5)^2−7\) to define \(f\).</para>
<para>Do you agree with either of them? </para>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>No. The vertex of the graph is \((5,-7)\). The graph of Andre’s function would have a vertex at \((5,7)\), and the graph of Noah’s function would have a vertex at \((-5,-7)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Here are the graphs of \(f(x)=x^2\), \(f(x)=x^2−5\), and \(f(x)=(x+2)^2−8\).</item>
</list>
<para> <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b28e72de3d5826c42cdf5b308f3eff1a1537d56c"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> How do the three graphs compare?
      <list>
        <item> They all have the same vertex. </item>
        <item> The shape is the same but they are in different locations. </item>
        <item> They all have a negative a coefficient in the vertex form of the equation. </item>
        <item> All three graphs contain the point \((0,0)\).  </item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The shape is the same but they are in different locations.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> Compare the graphs of \(f(x)=x^2\) and \(f(x)=x^2−5\). What role does the -5 play in the comparison?
      <list>
        <item> Subtracting 5 from the squared term shifts the graph left by 5 units. </item>
        <item> Subtracting 5 from the squared term shifts the graph right by 5 units. </item>
        <item> Subtracting 5 from the squared term shifts the graph up by 5 units. </item>
        <item> Subtracting 5 from the squared term shifts the graph down by 5 units. </item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Subtracting 5 from the squared term shifts the graph down by 5 units.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item> Compare the graphs of \(f(x)=x^2\) and \(f(x)=(x+2)^2−8\). What roles do the \(+2\) and \(-8\) play in the comparison?
      <list>
        <item> Adding 2 to \(x\) before squaring shifts the graph of \(f(x)=x^2\) to the left by 2 units and subtracting 8 from the squared term shifts the graph down by 8 units. </item>
        <item> Adding 2 to \(x\) before squaring shifts the graph of \(f(x)=x^2\) to the up by 2 units and subtracting 8 from the squared term shifts the graph left by 8 units. </item>
        <item> Adding 2 to \(x\) before squaring shifts the graph of \(f(x)=x^2\) to the right by 2 units and subtracting 8 from the squared term shifts the graph up by 8 units. </item>
        <item> Adding 2 to \(x\) before squaring shifts the graph of \(f(x)=x^2\) to the down by 2 units and subtracting 8 from the squared term shifts the graph right by 8 units. </item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Adding 2 to \(x\) before squaring shifts the graph of \(f(x)=x^2\) to the left by 2 units and subtracting 8 from the squared term shifts the graph down by 8 units.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item><emphasis>Select the three </emphasis>equations with a graph whose vertex has both a positive \(x\) and a positive \(y\).  </item>
</list>
    <list>
      <item> \(f(x)=x^2\) </item>
      <item> \(f(x)=(x−1)^2\) </item>
      <item> \(f(x)=(x−3)^2+2\)</item>
      <item> \(f(x)=2(x−4)^2−5\) </item>
      <item> \(f(x)=0.5(x+2)^2+6\) </item>
      <item> \(f(x)=-(x−4)^2+3\) </item>
      <item> \(f(x)=-2(x−3)^2+1\) </item>
    </list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(f(x)=(x−3)^2+2\), \(f(x)=-(x−4)^2+3\), \(f(x)=-2(x−3)^2+1\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which equation represents the graph shown?
    
    <figure><media alt="GRAPH OF A DOWNWARD PARABOLA WITH A VERTEX AT (NEGATIVE 7, 3"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e4e10406bde154391ac0790d52a32789409c6a48"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
    <list>
      <item>\(f(x)=-(x+7)^2+3\) </item>
      <item> \(f(x)=-(x-7)^2+3\) </item>
      <item> \(f(x)=(x+7)^2+3\) </item>
      <item> \(f(x)=(x-7)^2+3\) </item>
    </list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(f(x)=-(x+7)^2+3\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>The graph representing \(f(x)=x^2\) is shifted 4 units to the left, 16 units down, and flipped so it opens downward (reflects over the \(x\)-axis). Which equation defines the curve?  </item>
</list>
    <list>
      <item> \(f(x)=(x+4)^2-16\) </item>
      <item> \(f(x)=-(x-4)^2-16\) </item>
      <item> \(f(x)=-(x+4)^2-16\) </item>
      <item> \(f(x)=(x-4)^2-16\) </item>
    </list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(f(x)=-(x+4)^2-16\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>The graph of \(f(x) = x^2\) is transformed by a horizontal dilation of \(\frac12\). What is the equation of the function that results from this translation? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(f(x) =\frac 12 x^2\)</item>
  <item>\(f(x) = x^2 + \frac12\)</item>
  <item>\(f(x) = (x-\frac 12)^2\)</item>
  <item>\(f(x) = (\frac12x)^2\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(f(x) = (\frac12x)^2\) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>A function \(g(x) = (2x)^2 - 5\) was transformed from the parent quadratic function. Which of the following answer choices describes the transformations that were applied? </item>
</list>
<para>Select the<emphasis> two</emphasis> descriptions that apply.</para>

<list>
  <item>A horizontal shift 2 units to the right</item>
  <item>A horizontal dilation by a factor of 2</item>
  <item>A horizontal shift left 5 units</item>
  <item>A vertical shift down 5 units</item>
  <item>A vertical dilation by a factor of 2</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> A horizontal dilation by a factor of 2 and A vertical shift down 5 units</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which of the following represents a function that has experienced a horizontal compression? </item>
</list>

<list>
  <item>\(f(x) = (-1x)^2\)</item>
  <item>\(f(x) = (\frac 15x)^2\)</item>
  <item>\(f(x) = (\frac 52x)^2\)</item>
  <item>\(f(x) = x^2\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(f(x) = (\frac 52x)^2\)</para>

